        "33": {
            "annotation": "ZERO-HIT GUARD (Assembly (Branch B)). ABRicate's own summary_table() has a known edge case (github.com/tseemann/abricate, single-input-file 'dutch mode'): the safeguard that initializes an empty row 'so it appears in the report even if 0 genes found' is explicitly skipped in dutch mode (`$data{$fname} = {} unless $dutch_mode`), so a single-file summary with 0 ARG hits degenerates to a bare '#FILE\\tNUM_FOUND' header with NO data row at all. That specific shape (gene/placeholder columns present, 0 data rows) crashes table_compute's downstream transpose+to_csv(quoting=QUOTE_NONE) with '_csv.Error: single empty field record must be quoted' (confirmed by direct local reproduction, 2026-07-11 real-run failure). This step detects that exact degenerate line and rewrites it to '#FILE\\nno_ARGs_detected\\n' (0 gene columns, 1 placeholder row) — the one shape that transposes and writes cleanly. Normal (non-degenerate) input passes through completely unchanged.",
            "content_id": "toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu/repos/bgruening/text_processing/tp_awk_tool/1.1.2",
            "errors": null,
            "id": 33,
            "input_connections": {
                "infile": {
                    "id": 11,
                    "output_name": "summary"
                }
            },
            "inputs": [],
            "label": "Zero-hit guard — Assembly (Branch B)",
            "name": "Text reformatting with awk",
            "outputs": [
                {
                    "name": "outfile",
                    "type": "tabular"
                }
            ],
            "position": {
                "left": 1440,
                "top": 650
            },
            "post_job_actions": {
                "RenameDatasetActionoutfile": {
                    "action_type": "RenameDatasetAction",
                    "output_name": "outfile",
                    "action_arguments": {
                        "newname": "IPK2026_33_ABRSummary_Assembly_ZeroHitGuard.tabular"
                    }
                }
            },
            "tool_id": "toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu/repos/bgruening/text_processing/tp_awk_tool/1.1.2",
            "tool_shed_repository": {
                "changeset_revision": "",
                "name": "text_processing",
                "owner": "bgruening",
                "tool_shed": "toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu"
            },
            "tool_state": "{\"infile\": {\"__class__\": \"ConnectedValue\"}, \"code\": \"{ lines[NR] = $0 }\\nEND {\\n  if (NR == 1 && lines[1] ~ /^#FILE\\\\tNUM_FOUND$/) {\\n    print \\\"#FILE\\\"\\n    print \\\"no_ARGs_detected\\\"\\n  } else {\\n    for (i = 1; i <= NR; i++) print lines[i]\\n  }\\n}\", \"__page__\": null, \"__rerun_remap_job_id__\": null}",
            "tool_uuid": null,
            "tool_version": "1.1.2",
            "type": "tool",
            "uuid": "906ea489-61fb-40de-bdc2-89a9deda4121",
            "when": null,
            "workflow_outputs": []
        },
        "34": {
            "annotation": "2026-07-15 fix: actually decompress Pfam-A.hmm.gz. The previous annotation on Step 25 claimed \"HMMER 3.4 handles .gz transparently\" — that was never verified and is wrong. Uses Galaxy's built-in CONVERTER_gz_to_uncompressed (gzip -dcf). [2026-07-17 fix: this hidden converter tool writes its own dynamic output name via a configfile/galaxy.json mechanism (input name minus '.gz' + ' uncompressed'), which overrides workflow-level RenameDatasetAction — confirmed by a real run where the symlink was created as './pfam/Pfam-A.hmm uncompressed' instead of './pfam/Pfam-A.hmm', which BiG-SCAPE's hardcoded hmmpress call then couldn't find. Rename/datatype-change moved to the new pass-through step (Step 35) instead, where RenameDatasetAction is already proven to work reliably elsewhere in this pipeline (e.g. the original __EXTRACT_DATASET__ step).]",
            "content_id": "CONVERTER_gz_to_uncompressed",
            "errors": null,
            "id": 34,
            "input_connections": {
